First-wave sources are the eight below. The first five papers, in order, are in `PLAN.md` and `papers/index.qmd`. Read the license. Do not commit raw tracking dumps, full Statcast extracts, or the StatsBomb tree.

Files without a clear license are linked at a pinned commit and fetched at build or run time, never copied into the repo.

License notes for the last four were read from each source's own page on 2 November 2026. Pins are the commits already in that paper's `DATA.md`.

1. StatsBomb open data (https://github.com/statsbomb/open-data). Follow the license in that repository. Credit StatsBomb as that license requires.
2. A named NFL Big Data Bowl release. Follow that release's data terms, link it in `DATA.md`, and name it on the page. Do not commit the tracking files.
3. nflverse (https://github.com/nflverse). nflverse-data is CC-BY-4.0. Credit: `Play-by-play data: nflverse (github.com/nflverse/nflverse-data), licensed CC-BY-4.0.`
4. Baseball Savant (https://baseballsavant.mlb.com/). MLB's terms. Do not commit a bulk download. Credit: `Pitch data: MLB Advanced Media via Baseball Savant (baseballsavant.mlb.com).`
5. Penalty-kick authors' repository (https://github.com/sdsander-syr/soccer_pk_shot_location), commit `ff98e29120d8597a6c6b21670fbe43ae7dc816b4`. No license is stated. Credit: `Penalty data: Uribe, Sanders, Ehrlich, Reade & Singleton (2025), github.com/sdsander-syr/soccer_pk_shot_location.`
6. OptimalDarts (https://github.com/wangchunsem/OptimalDarts), commit `f68bedcc592b4bcfda69f6d72d69aede91ebaff1`. No license is stated (`readme.txt` at that commit states none). Credit: `Skill models: M. B. Haugh & C. Wang, 'Play like the pros? Solving the game of darts as a dynamic zero sum game' (github.com/wangchunsem/OptimalDarts).` Do not paste the repository's code into `code/`.
7. Metrica Sports sample tracking data (https://github.com/metrica-sports/sample-data), commit `e706dd506b360d69d9d123d5b8026e7294b13996`. No license file. The README "Legal stuff" section states: "Please be responsible with the use of this data." and "If you use it for anything public, please aknowledge the source." Credit: `Tracking and event data: Metrica Sports sample data (github.com/metrica-sports/sample-data). Players and teams are anonymized.`
8. Retrosheet (https://www.retrosheet.org/notice.txt). The notice allows any desired use, including a commercial product, if this statement appears prominently. Use it verbatim as the credit line:

```
     The information used here was obtained free of
     charge from and is copyrighted by Retrosheet.  Interested
     parties may contact Retrosheet at "www.retrosheet.org".
```

Every paper has `DATA.md` with `substitute: yes` or `substitute: no`, the paper's source, our source, the license, and the sample.

- `substitute: no` means the page uses the paper's own public files. Do not show the substitute callout.
- `substitute: yes` means the page must include a warning callout with class `substitute-data` and the heading "Rebuilt on substitute data." Name both datasets, what transfers, and what will not match the paper.

Public data can still be substitute data. Tracking rebuilt on event data is a substitute. nflverse in place of an author-built play-by-play file is a substitute. Baseball Savant in place of a private Statcast extract is a substitute unless the brief shows the files are the same. Metrica sample tracking in place of private TRACAB is a substitute.

Redraw paper figures, or reproduce them only when the license allows. Cite the figure number.
